# Java/Kotlin + Spring security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to Spring Boot
3.x-specific risks (OWASP). Broadly scoped to all Java/Kotlin sources for
data-access and secrets guidance, with extra emphasis on controller and
security-config files for the HTTP-facing rules.

## Spring Security configuration

- A `SecurityFilterChain` `@Bean` from a `@Configuration` class
  (`http.build()`); never extend `WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter` (removed in
  Spring Security 6 / Spring Boot 3).
- Scope authorization explicitly (`authorizeHttpRequests` with path/role
  matchers); no catch-all `permitAll()` left from one development endpoint.

## CSRF

- A stateless, token-authenticated API (bearer JWT/OAuth2, no session
  cookie) may disable CSRF (`csrf(AbstractHttpConfigurer::disable)`); state
  the reason when disabling it.
- A session-cookie-authenticated endpoint (browser form login, `JSESSIONID`)
  keeps CSRF protection enabled.

## SQL and data access

- Spring Data JPA derived queries or parameterized `@Query` (named or
  positional bind parameters); never string-concatenated or
  `String.format`-built SQL/JPQL with a user-influenced value.
- `JdbcTemplate`/`NamedParameterJdbcTemplate` through its parameter-binding
  API, never interpolation into the SQL string.

## Input validation as the DTO boundary defense

- Validate untrusted input at the DTO boundary with `jakarta.validation`
  annotations plus `@Valid`/`@Validated` on the controller method, in
  addition to parameterized queries downstream.

## Secrets and configuration

- No hard-coded credential, API key, or signing secret in source or in
  committed `application.yml`/`application.properties`; use environment
  variables, a config server, or the project's secret store.
- Do not log a request/response body that may carry credentials, tokens, or
  PII at `INFO`/`DEBUG` without redaction.

## Dependency hygiene

- Run the project's configured dependency-vulnerability check (OWASP
  Dependency-Check plugin, `./gradlew dependencyCheckAnalyze`) when
  introducing or bumping a dependency, including a minor bump.
